<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Koine Greek</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as koine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Koine Greek was the common form of Greek that spread across the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> after the conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. It served as the language of administration, commerce and culture in the Hellenistic kingdoms, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>. Its spread was one of the most lasting legacies of Seleucid rule, and it later became the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koine-greek.jpg" alt="Koine Greek" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gary Todd from Xinzheng, China · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greek_Script_from_Rosetta_Stone_(36140196440).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Koine, from a Greek phrase meaning the common dialect, arose within the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. Under Macedonian leadership this common variety was spoken from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, and it replaced the older Greek dialects with an everyday form that people anywhere could understand. The post-classical period of Greek is defined as beginning with Alexander's death in 323 BCE. Ancient Macedonian contributed some military terms and changes in grammar to the new language.</p><p>Its roots were unclear even in antiquity. Hellenistic scholars thought it a mixture of the four main dialects, a view Paul Kretschmer supported in 1901, while Ulrich von Wilamowitz-Moellendorff and Antoine Meillet saw a simplified Ionic. Most scholars now follow Georgios Hatzidakis, who showed that its stable nucleus is Attic with admixtures, above all from Ionic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Koine ran from conservative literary styles to the spoken vernaculars of the time. Literary Koine was the medium of much post-classical writing, including the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, and the Roman emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> wrote his private thoughts in it in the work now called Meditations. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>'s eastern provinces local elites and communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> used Greek for inscriptions, documents and official announcements, even where it was not the main spoken language. A decree of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> to the town of Thisbae in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> in 170 BCE survives in it.</p><p>Its sounds changed greatly over the period. From the second century BCE the distinction between long and short vowels was lost and a stress accent replaced the ancient pitch accent. Several diphthongs became single vowels, and pronunciation moved from close to Ancient Greek towards Modern Greek.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Koine is the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a>, the Greek translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> made in the 3rd century BCE, of the Christian New Testament and of most early Christian theological writing by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-fathers">Church Fathers</a>, and in this setting it is called Biblical, New Testament or patristic Greek. Scholars have debated how far Biblical Greek reflects ordinary spoken Koine and how far <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> influence shaped it. As the dominant language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> it developed into Medieval Greek, which informed Modern Greek, and it continues as the liturgical language of the Greek Orthodox Church and some Greek Catholic churches.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Koine Greek” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Attic dialect became the basis of the <b>koine</b>, the common Greek of the Hellenistic world and of the New Testament.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek language, art and philosophy spread across Egypt, Mesopotamia, Persia and Central Asia, influencing government, literature and science for centuries, and <b>Koine Greek</b> became a common language of administration and commerce.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Seleucid Empire spread Hellenistic Greek culture, its language, urban planning, philosophy, art and science, across the arc of territory from the Mediterranean coast to the borders of India, creating a cultural <b>koine</b> that influenced every civilisation within its reach.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A common form of the language, the <b>Koine</b>, based on Attic, replaced the old dialects and was spoken as a first or second language by millions of people of many origins.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The result was the Hellenistic world: a vast zone in which a common form of Greek, the <b>koine</b>, became the language of government, commerce, and learning.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexander's conquests made Greek the language of government, trade and culture from Egypt to Afghanistan, and the common <b>Koine</b> replaced the old dialects.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-written-word">The Written Word</a></li></ul></section>
